  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a wide range of formulation materials and delivery systems on a logarithmic size scale in nanometres, spanning from small molecules to large devices. At the smallest end, it shows chemical excipients and modifiers such as piperazine derivatives, chelators, surfactants, ionic liquids, pH modifiers, protease inhibitors, and polymers. These appear to represent molecular additives used to improve stability, permeability, or protection of therapeutic cargo.\n\nMoving to larger scales, the image highlights self-assembled nanosystems including micelles and liposomes, followed by much larger capsules. Beneath the same size axis are other carrier platforms: inorganic nanoparticles, polymeric nanoparticles, hydrogels, biodegradable microneedles, protein crystals, and mucoadhesive patches. Their placement emphasizes how drug-delivery approaches differ greatly in physical dimensions, from sub-nanometre compounds to macroscopic patch-like systems approaching very large sizes.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that delivery strategies form a continuum rather than a single class of materials. Small chemical enhancers, nanoscale carriers, and bulk biomaterials all occupy distinct but overlapping size regimes, suggesting multiple complementary routes for formulating and administering biologics or other therapeutics.",
